{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. It focuses on outcomes with probabilities below 20%, which are subject to a weighting adjustment: their probability is increased by 10 percentage points. In Lottery A, all outcomes have 100% probability, so there are no outcomes below 20%—hence, no adjustment applies. In Lottery B, the 5000 Euro outcome has a 0.1% probability (well below 20%), so its weighted probability becomes 10.1% (0.1% + 10%). The 0 Euro outcome in B has 99.9% probability, so it is not a small-probability outcome and is not adjusted. The reason evaluates each lottery by the sum of outcomes weighted by their (adjusted) probabilities. For Lottery A: 5 × 1.0 = 5. For Lottery B: 5000 × 0.101 = 505. Thus, the decision reason would prefer Lottery B due to its much higher weighted expected value.",
